// compiler/optstrbox.bit — #4648: a `string` header the optimizer has already
// DECLINED to delete, whose three words are one call's result run, becomes
// #4628's reuse diamond instead of an allocation. Sibling of opt.bit in the
// same module, so nothing here needs an import — `blockEmissionOrder`,
// `emitTranslated`, `trKeepAlive`, `noConst` and the builder API are all
// defined elsewhere in this module, and `emitStringHandleReuse` in
// lowerexplodestr.bit is the diamond emitter this shares with the lowerer.
//
// ## The shape, and why no lowering placement reaches it
//
// `let k = pick(); Slot{key: k}` under the flip. `pick` hands its result back
// in three registers (#4424), `emitResolvedCall` re-boxes them, #4586's
// `stringLetExplodes` explodes that fresh box straight back into words, and
// then `Slot{key: k}` wants ONE HANDLE — so `localRead` builds a SECOND box
// with the identical contents. That second box is what this pass removes: 1000
// of them in `_tests_/bit/stringexplode`'s `plainretfielddirect`, which read
// 1017 objects gate-off against 2017 gate-on before this pass existed.
//
// #4628 measured all three lowering placements and rejected them, which is why
// this is a pass and not four more lines in lowerexplodestr.bit:
//
//   at `emitResolvedCall`         retscan 6 -> 18 objects
//   at every exploded local read  strings 37 -> 7,125,035 objects
//   at the RETURN                 landed; does not reach this shape
//
// The middle one is the whole difficulty. `localRead` is shared with every
// WORD consumer of an exploded local — `len(a)` and `explodeCallArgs` both
// materialize the aggregate and read words straight back out — and a diamond
// hands them a BLOCK PARAMETER, which store-to-load forwarding cannot cross.
// The box on the failing arm then runs whether or not anything wanted a handle.
//
// RUNNING AFTER THE FOLDS IS WHAT SEPARATES THE TWO CASES, and it does it by
// construction rather than by a predicate. A box whose consumers wanted words
// is already GONE by the time this pass looks: `forwardFieldLoads`
// (optstore.bit) answered each `field_get` from the `field_set` above it and
// `deadAllocs` (optalloc.bit) took the allocation nothing named. What survives
// to here is a box some consumer genuinely holds as a handle, so every fold
// this pass could have cost has already happened.
//
// ## What makes the rewrite sound
//
// Nothing new. It is #4628's argument verbatim, and it rests on the reuse TEST
// rather than on where word 2 came from: a `string` is immutable (SPEC §5.3),
// so a handle whose own `ptr`/`len` equal `{w0, w1}` denotes exactly the bytes
// `{w0, w1}` denote, and its own `base` roots them. `emitStringHandleReuse`
// emits that test — `w2 != nil && w2.ptr == w0 && w2.len == w1` — and the
// failing edge still allocates. #4619 built the guardless form and it
// miscompiled (`bench/cases/json` md5 `cc08f932` against `58d3db22`);
// `retbasereuse` in _tests_/bit/stringexplode is the fixture that reddens when
// the comparison is deleted — through the LOWERER's copy of the diamond, since
// the callee test below means this pass never reaches a site where the test
// can fail. The dynamic test is defence in depth HERE and the primary guard
// THERE, and `directbasereuse` is the fixture that catches losing both.
//
// ## What the CALLEE test is for, and why it is not a second safety net
//
// Both requirements below — word 2 is a call's third result word, and that
// call's target returns its OWN handle there — are about PROFIT. Correctness
// is the dynamic test's job and nothing here weakens it.
//
// THE CALLEE TEST EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS PASS COST CYCLES
// WHERE IT COULD NOT PAY. Rewriting every call-result box moved
// `bench/cases/json` flip-on by +2.77% median cycles (15 interleaved rounds,
// null control +0.29%) while its object count did not move one allocation:
// 5,400,067 before and after. Those sites take the FAILING edge every time —
// their callee returns `s[lo:hi]`, so word 2 is the owner base — and a failing
// diamond is the allocation it already had plus three comparisons.
//
// So the pass asks the callee. `retWordsAreOwnHandle` is the STATIC half of
// the same invariant the dynamic test checks: a `ret w0, w1, v` whose `w0` and
// `w1` are `field_get v[0]` and `field_get v[8]` hands back a handle that
// denotes its own bytes, so the caller's test succeeds — always, except on a
// null handle, where the `!= nil` arm allocates as it does today.
// `explodedRetWordValues`'s fresh-box guard is what makes the two shapes
// distinguishable at all: `return xs[i]` gives `ret field_get %v[0],
// field_get %v[8], %v` and `return s[lo:hi]` gives `ret add %p, %lo, ...`.
//
// ## Gated OFF for every real build
//
// `optimizeModule` calls this only when `stringExplodeEnabled()`, so with
// `BIT_STRING_EXPLODE_TEST` unset not one function is even scanned and the IR
// is byte-identical instruction for instruction — the same requirement #4628
// put first among `retHandleFor`'s three guards, and for the same reason: a
// flip-off reading that moves is the proof that a gate leaked.

// Whether `ins` allocates a `string` header — ABI.md §2.3's 24 bytes with the
// single traced word at +16, as `boxStringWords` (lowerexplodestr.bit) and
// `emitInlineStringSlice` (lowerreslice.bit) both build it.
//
// THE IR TYPE IS THE AUTHORITY, not the size and not the offsets.
// `explodedWordTypes` gives a tuple `(i64, i64, string)` the same three word
// types a `string` gets, and `buildTupleIn` lays that tuple out at the same 24
// bytes with the same `ptr_offsets` — so a rule keyed on the shape alone would
// hand a caller of a tuple-returning function the tuple's third member where it
// expects the tuple, which is #4412's exact failure. `ty` is what
// `materializeExplodedIn` itself branches on.
fn isStringHeaderAlloc(ctx: TypeContext, ins: IrInstr): bool {
  return ins.op == Op.GcAlloc &&
    ins.ty == primType(ctx, 11) &&
    ins.ival == strViewHeaderSize() &&
    len(ins.offsets) == 1 &&
    ins.offsets[0] == strViewBaseOffset()
}

// Whether the three instructions after `idx` are that box's own `ptr`/`len`/
// `base` stores, in `stringWordTypes` order and inside the same block (`end` is
// the block's exclusive instruction end).
//
// Contiguous and immediately after, exactly as `freshStringBoxWords`
// (lowerexplodestr.bit) requires of the builder: `boxStringWords` emits the
// four in one run and no pass in `optimizeModule` inserts between them, so the
// tight test costs nothing real and FAILS CLOSED on any shape it has not seen.
fn stringBoxWordSets(f: IrFunc, idx: int, end: int): bool {
  if (idx + 3 >= end) {
    return false
  }
  let k = 1
  while (k < 4) {
    let fs = f.instrs[idx + k]
    if (fs.op != Op.FieldSet || fs.lhs != idx || fs.ival != (k - 1) * 8) {
      return false
    }
    k = k + 1
  }
  return true
}

// Whether `v` is word `ival` of `call`'s result run (#4405's `Op.CallWord`).
// `args[0]` is the ownership the run records; `callWordRunLen` (ir.bit) reads
// the same field rather than trusting adjacency, and so does this.
fn isCallWordOf(f: IrFunc, v: int, call: int, ival: int): bool {
  let ins = f.instrs[v]
  return ins.op == Op.CallWord && ins.ival == ival && len(ins.args) == 1 && ins.args[0] == call
}

// The call `w0`/`w1`/`w2` are the three result words of, or -1.
//
// Word 0 IS the call instruction — `%c = call @pick(...)` then
// `call_word %c[1]`, `call_word %c[2]` — which is how the arm64 backend binds
// the run (`emitCallWords`, arm64call.bit) and how the verifier polices it
// (`callWordEscapesInFunc`, verify.bit).
fn stringBoxCallOf(f: IrFunc, w0: int, w1: int, w2: int): int {
  if (!opIsCall(f.instrs[w0].op)) {
    return -1
  }
  if (!isCallWordOf(f, w1, w0, 1) || !isCallWordOf(f, w2, w0, 2)) {
    return -1
  }
  return w0
}

// `v` is `field_get base[off]`.
fn loadsFieldOf(g: IrFunc, v: int, base: int, off: int): bool {
  let ins = g.instrs[v]
  return ins.op == Op.FieldGet && ins.lhs == base && ins.ival == off
}

// Whether EVERY `ret` in `g` hands back a `string` whose word 2 is a handle its
// own words 0 and 1 were read out of — the static half of the reuse invariant,
// and this pass's profit test.
//
// One non-conforming `ret` refuses the whole function: a caller cannot know
// which one it will reach, and a diamond that fails is the allocation it
// already had plus three comparisons.
//
// A function with no `ret` at all (every path panics) refuses too — `seen`
// starts false — because there is nothing to have proven.
fn retWordsAreOwnHandle(ctx: TypeContext, g: IrFunc): bool {
  if (g.result != primType(ctx, 11)) {
    return false
  }
  let seen = false
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(g.instrs)) {
    let ins = g.instrs[i]
    if (ins.op == Op.Ret) {
      if (len(ins.args) != 3 || !loadsFieldOf(g, ins.args[0], ins.args[2], 0)) {
        return false
      }
      if (!loadsFieldOf(g, ins.args[1], ins.args[2], 8)) {
        return false
      }
      seen = true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return seen
}

// Whether the call at `c` resolves to a function in `snap` whose returns are
// all reuse-friendly. An unresolvable target — a `call_value`, an interface
// dispatch, an `rt_call`, or a name this module does not hold — refuses.
//
// `snap` is the module's function list as it stood BEFORE this pass rewrote
// anything, the same discipline #3163 gave the inliner: a pass that reads
// callees out of the list it is halfway through mutating gets an answer that
// depends on function order.
fn callReturnsOwnHandle(ctx: TypeContext, snap: []IrFunc, f: IrFunc, c: int): bool {
  if (f.instrs[c].op != Op.Call) {
    return false
  }
  let gi = funcIndexByName(snap, f.instrs[c].name)
  if (gi < 0) {
    return false
  }
  return retWordsAreOwnHandle(ctx, snap[gi])
}

// Whether the `gc_alloc` at `idx` is a re-box of one call's three result words,
// and that call's target puts its own handle in word 2.
fn reusableStringBox(x: StrBoxScan, f: IrFunc, idx: int, end: int): bool {
  if (!isStringHeaderAlloc(x.ctx, f.instrs[idx]) || !stringBoxWordSets(f, idx, end)) {
    return false
  }
  let c = stringBoxCallOf(
    f, f.instrs[idx + 1].rhs, f.instrs[idx + 2].rhs, f.instrs[idx + 3].rhs,
  )
  if (c < 0) {
    return false
  }
  return callReturnsOwnHandle(x.ctx, x.snap, f, c)
}

// The two module-wide facts the scan consults, bundled so the walk stays inside
// E0202's five-parameter limit. Both are READ-ONLY for the whole pass.
class StrBoxScan {
  ctx: TypeContext,
  snap: []IrFunc,
}

// Marks every reusable box in block `bi`.
fn markReusableInBlock(x: StrBoxScan, f: IrFunc, mask: []bool, bi: int) {
  let blk = f.blocks[bi]
  let end = blk.instsStart + blk.instsLen
  let idx = blk.instsStart + blk.paramCount
  while (idx < end) {
    if (reusableStringBox(x, f, idx, end)) {
      mask[idx] = true
      idx = idx + 4
    } else {
      idx = idx + 1
    }
  }
}

// Unmarks any box some OTHER instruction writes into, so the three words this
// pass reads off the construction run are the whole of what the box holds.
//
// A `field_set` at a `string` base cannot occur today — strings are immutable
// (SPEC §5.3) and `boxStringWords` is the only builder of this shape — so this
// walk removes nothing on any input this compiler currently produces. It is
// here because the alternative is a comment asserting that, and a later pass
// that starts rewriting header words would turn the assertion into a silently
// wrong answer rather than a refusal.
fn dropWrittenStringBoxes(f: IrFunc, mask: []bool) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(f.instrs)) {
    let ins = f.instrs[i]
    if (ins.op == Op.FieldSet && mask[ins.lhs] && (i < ins.lhs + 1 || i > ins.lhs + 3)) {
      mask[ins.lhs] = false
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Per instruction, whether it is a `gc_alloc` this pass replaces with the reuse
// diamond. Two bounded walks: one over the blocks, one over the instructions.
fn reusableStringBoxes(x: StrBoxScan, f: IrFunc): []bool {
  let mask = noDeadAllocs(len(f.instrs))
  let bi = 0
  while (bi < len(f.blocks)) {
    markReusableInBlock(x, f, mask, bi)
    bi = bi + 1
  }
  dropWrittenStringBoxes(f, mask)
  return mask
}

fn anyMarked(mask: []bool): bool {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(mask)) {
    if (mask[i]) {
      return true
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// The rewrite state for one function, bundled the way `AppendCtx`
// (optappend.bit) and `RebuildCtx` (optrebuild.bit) are, so the walk stays
// inside E0202's five-parameter limit.
class StrBoxCtx {
  b: IrBuilder,
  f: IrFunc,
  ctx: TypeContext,
  remap: []int,
  blockMap: []int,
  known: []ConstVal,
  mask: []bool,
}

// Replaces the box at `idx` with the diamond, and yields the join parameter —
// the value the box's own id must remap to.
//
// The words are read off the box's three `field_set`s and translated through
// `remap`, never re-loaded with `field_get`s: the old box is then named by
// nothing at all, which is what lets it not be emitted rather than left for a
// later pass to prove dead.
//
// The span is the ALLOCATION's, #3282's span-on-rewrite policy — the diamond
// replaces that instruction, and `builderEmit` would otherwise stamp the
// builder's unset `curSpan` onto every instruction in it.
fn emitReusedStringBox(x: StrBoxCtx, idx: int): int {
  let vals = [
    x.remap[x.f.instrs[idx + 1].rhs],
    x.remap[x.f.instrs[idx + 2].rhs],
    x.remap[x.f.instrs[idx + 3].rhs],
  ]
  builderSetSpan(x.b, x.f.instrs[idx].span)
  let v = emitStringHandleReuse(x.ctx, x.b, vals)
  builderSetSpan(x.b, unknownSpan())
  return v
}

// One block of `f`, rewritten into `b`. A rewritten box closes the block being
// written and opens the diamond's join, and the rest of the block — its
// terminator included — lands there, exactly as `expandAppendBlock` splits a
// block around an expanded append.
fn rewriteStringBoxBlock(x: StrBoxCtx, bi: int) {
  let blk = x.f.blocks[bi]
  builderBeginBlock(x.b, x.blockMap[bi])
  let idx = blk.instsStart
  let paramsEnd = blk.instsStart + blk.paramCount
  while (idx < paramsEnd) {
    x.remap[idx] = builderAddParam(x.b, x.f.instrs[idx].ty)
    idx = idx + 1
  }
  let end = blk.instsStart + blk.instsLen
  while (idx < end) {
    if (x.mask[idx]) {
      x.remap[idx] = emitReusedStringBox(x, idx)
      // The three `field_set`s are the box; they leave with it. Nothing can
      // name a `void` store, so their `remap` slots stay unread.
      idx = idx + 4
    } else {
      emitTranslated(x.b, x.f, x.known, x.remap, x.blockMap, idx)
      idx = idx + 1
    }
  }
  builderEndBlock(x.b)
}

// The `IrFunc` with every marked box replaced. Block ids are minted for the old
// blocks in emission order and the diamond's four are minted as they are
// reached, so they do NOT ascend with the linear schedule — `reuseStringBoxes`
// runs `deadCodeElim` afterwards for exactly that reason.
fn rewriteStringBoxes(ctx: TypeContext, f: IrFunc, mask: []bool): IrFunc {
  let n = len(f.blocks)
  let blockMap = []int(0)
  let remap = []int(0)
  let known = []ConstVal(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    blockMap = append(blockMap, 0)
    i = i + 1
  }
  i = 0
  while (i < len(f.instrs)) {
    remap = append(remap, 0)
    known = append(known, noConst())
    i = i + 1
  }
  let order = blockEmissionOrder(f)
  let b = newBuilder()
  i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    blockMap[order[i]] = builderNewBlock(b)
    i = i + 1
  }
  let x = StrBoxCtx{
    b: b, f: f, ctx: ctx, remap: remap, blockMap: blockMap, known: known, mask: mask,
  }
  i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    rewriteStringBoxBlock(x, order[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  return IrFunc{
    name: f.name, paramTypes: f.paramTypes, result: f.result,
    blocks: b.blocks, instrs: b.instrs, isFallible: f.isFallible,
    isNaked: f.isNaked, isNosplit: f.isNosplit,
    // §17.6 module tag, §11.9 pin flag and #4406's keepalive edges ride every
    // rebuild — see `rebuild`'s own note (optrebuild.bit) for what dropping any
    // of them costs. A pair naming a rewritten box translates to the join
    // parameter, which is a `string` handle rooting the same bytes.
    inRootModule: f.inRootModule, isPinned: f.isPinned,
    keepAlive: trKeepAlive(f.keepAlive, remap),
  }
}

// The pass: `f` with its reusable string headers replaced, or `f` itself when
// there are none — so a function this has no work in cannot be perturbed.
//
// THE `deadCodeElim` IS THE RENUMBER, not a cleanup. `rewriteStringBoxes` mints
// the diamond's blocks after every old block's id, so the ids no longer ascend
// with the linear schedule — the invariant `isBackEdge` and the x64 linear-scan
// allocator's interval numbering both assume (build.bit's `writeModuleExe`).
// `rebuild` re-assigns them in ascending `instsStart` order (optrebuild.bit's
// `blockEmissionOrder`), which is the same debt `expandAppends` settles by
// running before the second fold. This pass cannot run there — it needs the
// folds' answer — so it settles it itself, on the touched function only.
fn reuseStringBoxes(x: StrBoxScan, f: IrFunc, debug: bool): IrFunc {
  let mask = reusableStringBoxes(x, f)
  if (!anyMarked(mask)) {
    return f
  }
  return deadCodeElim(rewriteStringBoxes(x.ctx, f, mask), debug)
}

// Pass 6 of `optimizeModule`, and it runs ONLY under the string flip: with
// `BIT_STRING_EXPLODE_TEST` unset no function is scanned, so a real build's IR
// is untouched instruction for instruction.
fn reuseStringBoxesInModule(m: IrModule, debug: bool) {
  if (!stringExplodeEnabled()) {
    return
  }
  let snap = []IrFunc(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(m.funcs)) {
    snap = append(snap, m.funcs[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  let x = StrBoxScan{ ctx: m.ctx, snap: snap }
  i = 0
  while (i < len(m.funcs)) {
    m.funcs[i] = reuseStringBoxes(x, m.funcs[i], debug)
    i = i + 1
  }
}
